\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Every polynomial has finite absolute Gaussian moments}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{Let #{Q} be positive self-adjoint on a finite real inner-product space
#{(E,h)}, with weight #{w_Q=e^{-h(x,Qx)/2}} and canonical volume.
Every actual [polynomial function](fdip-002V) satisfies
##{\int_E |P(x)|w_Q(x)\,d\lambda_E(x)<\infty.}
This holds for every finite product and power of polynomials, including
#{P^2}, and therefore also for integration against the normalized
[law](fdip-0005).}
\proof{
\p{First take an integer #{k\geq0} and #{a>0}. Put #{c=a/4}, choose
an integer #{M\geq k/2}, and set #{C=\max(1,M!c^{-M})}.
For #{|x|\geq1}, the exponential series gives
#{e^{cx^2}\geq (cx^2)^M/M!}, hence
##{|x|^k e^{-a x^2/2}\leq M!c^{-M} e^{-a x^2/4}.}
For #{|x|\leq1}, the left side is at most #{e^{-a x^2/2}}, which is
at most #{e^{-a x^2/4}}. The global bound therefore holds with #{C}
and includes #{k=M=0}. Its right side is integrable by the
[scalar lemma](fdip-002S) with parameter #{a/2}.}
\p{In an [orthonormal eigenchart](fdip-002R), write
##{P(x)=\sum_\alpha c_\alpha\prod_i y_i^{\alpha_i},
\qquad w_Q(x)=\prod_i e^{-q_i y_i^2/2}.}
There are finitely many nonnegative-integer multiindices #{\alpha}.
Nonnegative [Tonelli](fdip-002Q) factors the weighted absolute integral of
each monomial into finite scalar integrals just proved. The triangle
inequality bounds the polynomial integral by the finite sum of these
integrals multiplied by #{|c_\alpha|}. Products and powers remain
polynomials, so the same argument applies. In dimension zero each
function is constant and its integral is its absolute value at zero.}
}
\p{The envelope estimate and finite-sum argument are direct deductions
from \citet{Section 2, PDF pp. 1-2}{conradGaussianIntegral} and
\citet{Theorem 1.7.15, pp. 200-201, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.
The orthogonal reduction agrees with
\citet{Lemma B.54, p. 504}{friedli2017statistical}.
TauCeti's \leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.integrable_eval_mul_gaussianEnvelope}
supports the one-variable polynomial envelope with mean zero and
variance #{w=1/a>0}. The finite products, coordinate substitution and
algebra closure above are separate arguments; no inverse-covariance
identity is used.}
